  "evaluation_context": "Python 3.11 with Inspect AI library for agentic evaluation; LangChain components (ChatOpenAI, ChatPromptTemplate) for LLM interactions; temperature=0.0 for reproducibility; offline environment with stateful REPL-like loop (max 10 turns); restricted toolset: file_read(path), python_exec(code) with conservative regex-based destructive pattern tagging, file_write(path, content) with path-sensitive monitoring",
  "limitations": "Evidence pack limited to abstract, introduction, and methodology sections. Quantitative experimental results, declaration-action gap metrics, livelock metrics, and cross-model prevalence data are in later sections not visible in this Evidence Pack. Post-hoc simulation results showing Action-Aware Supervision Layer interception capabilities are in unreferenced sections. The python_exec environment uses non-blocking regex-based logging rather than hard enforcement, which may not reflect production safety requirements.",
  "project_relevance": "Directly relevant to Agent semantic acceleration: Operational Hallucination manifests as persistent repetitive tool calls (livelocks), directly addressing the topic's question about reducing repeated Read/Grep/tool calls. The paper's livelock metrics and declaration-action gap provide quantitative evidence of inefficient tool use patterns. The Action-Aware Supervision Layer's runtime state tracking and forced termination primitives offer architectural mechanisms to prevent redundant tool execution, potentially reducing end-to-end latency and token consumption. The framework's focus on multi-turn tool execution chain optimization aligns with the direction's queries about agent runtime and tool-call reduction.",
